  "meta": {
    "title": "Only finitely many aa’s",
    "blurb": "FG ¬(a ∧ X a): from some point on, never two a’s in a row. Under co-Büchi the ringed state is the bad event, and a run accepts when it happens only finitely often. A DBA cannot recognise this language; a DcoBA does it in three states.",
    "inputs": [
      {
        "w": "(b)",
        "expect": "accept"
      },
      {
        "w": "(ab)",
        "expect": "accept"
      },
      {
        "w": "aaaa(ab)",
        "expect": "accept",
        "label": "aa only at the start"
      },
      {
        "w": "(abb)",
        "expect": "accept"
      },
      {
        "w": "(a)",
        "expect": "reject"
      },
      {
        "w": "(aab)",
        "expect": "reject"
      },
      {
        "w": "b(aba)",
        "expect": "reject",
        "label": "wraps round: …a|a…"
      }
    ],
    "library": {
      "author": {
        "login": "thethinkmachine"
      },
      "license": "CC-BY-4.0",
      "tags": [
        "ltl",
        "co-buchi",
        "persistence"
      ],
      "difficulty": "intermediate",
      "readme": "The state **aa** is entered every time the word has just shown two a's in a row. Co-Büchi acceptance reads the ring as \"must eventually stop\": the run accepts when inf(r) ∩ F = ∅.\n\nThis is the eventual version of **Never two a's in a row** (a safety property, in the DBA and DWA entries): a word may start with **aaaa**, as long as the pattern dies out. Languages of the form FG φ — *persistence* properties — are exactly where co-Büchi is natural and deterministic Büchi fails; compare **Eventually always b**."
    }
  }
}
